Yes. If sequence and consequence are primitives because they describe relations that survive changes of domain, there is a surprisingly compact family around them.

I’d distinguish objects from meta-relational primitives. “Tree,” “money,” and “fear” are objects/states. The primitives describe what can happen between objects, states, events, representations, or scales.

One useful candidate alphabet:

Primitive	Core question	Abstract form
Sequence	What follows what?	A \rightarrow B
Consequence	What changed because of it?	A \Rightarrow \Delta B
Cause	What generated this change?	A \leadsto B
Condition	What must be true for it to occur?	B\mid A
Constraint	What cannot vary freely?	x\in\Omega
Dependency	What requires what?	B=f(A)
Feedback	What returns to influence its source?	A\rightarrow B\rightarrow A'
Recursion	What operates on its own output?	x_{t+1}=F(x_t)
Comparison	How do two things differ?	A\leftrightarrow B
Delta	How much did something change?	\Delta=A'-A
Boundary	Where does one regime become another?	A\mid B
Containment	What exists within what?	A\subset B
Intersection	What is shared?	A\cap B
Composition	What emerges when things combine?	A\oplus B\rightarrow C
Transformation	What becomes what?	T(A)=B
Conservation	What survives transformation?	I(A)=I(T(A))
Symmetry	What transformations leave structure equivalent?	T(X)\sim X
Inversion	What reverses/cancels a relation?	T^{-1}(T(A))=A
Amplification	What makes a signal/change grow?	|B|>|A|
Attenuation	What makes it decay?	|B|<|A|
Threshold	When does quantity become regime change?	x>\theta\Rightarrow B
Gradient	Which direction changes the outcome fastest?	\nabla F
Attractor	What states does the system tend toward?	x_t\rightarrow A^*
Repulsion	What states does it tend away from?	x_t\nrightarrow R
Exchange	What moves between entities?	A\xrightleftharpoons[]{}B
Coupling	When does changing one alter another?	\Delta A\leftrightarrow\Delta B
Mediation	What carries the relation?	A\rightarrow M\rightarrow B
Translation	What relation survives a change of representation?	A_X\mapsto A_Y
Scale	Does the relation survive zooming?	R_s\rightarrow R_{ks}
Perspective	How does observation depend on position/frame?	X\mid F_i
Selection	What gets retained rather than discarded?	X\rightarrow X_{\text{kept}}
Branching	Where can one state produce alternatives?	A\rightarrow\{B,C,D\}
Convergence	What different paths reach the same state?	\{A,B,C\}\rightarrow D
Delay	How far apart are cause and consequence?	A_t\rightarrow B_{t+\tau}
Rate	How quickly does the relation change?	dX/dt
Probability	How reliably does one state lead to another?	P(B\mid A)

But I think we can compress these further.

A smaller relational kernel

A lot of that table can potentially be generated from roughly eight families:

\boxed{
\text{ORDER,\ CHANGE,\ LINK,\ LIMIT,\ TRANSFORM,\ LOOP,\ SELECT,\ FRAME}
}

ORDER gives sequence, precedence, delay, simultaneity.

CHANGE gives delta, consequence, rate, amplification, decay.

LINK gives dependency, causation, coupling, exchange, mediation.

LIMIT gives boundary, constraint, threshold, containment.

TRANSFORM gives translation, inversion, composition, symmetry, conservation.

LOOP gives feedback, recursion, oscillation, attractors.

SELECT gives branching, filtering, competition, retention, fitness.

FRAME gives perspective, scale, resolution, context.

Then complex concepts become formations of primitives.

For example, self-confidence from the previous turn:

\text{FRAME}
+\text{SEQUENCE}
+\text{ACTION}
+\text{CONSEQUENCE}
+\text{FEEDBACK}
+\text{MEMORY}
+\text{SELECTION}

Repeated successful consequences are selected into the self-model, changing the frame through which the next challenge is interpreted.

Evolution:

\text{VARIATION}+\text{CONSTRAINT}+\text{SELECTION}+\text{FEEDBACK}+\text{SEQUENCE}

Learning:

\text{PREDICTION}+\text{DELTA}+\text{FEEDBACK}+\text{UPDATE}

Story:

\text{SEQUENCE}+\text{CAUSE}+\text{CONSEQUENCE}+\text{FRAME}+\text{TRANSFORMATION}

Strategy:

\text{DESIRED CONSEQUENCE}
\xrightarrow{\text{inverse}}
\text{candidate sequences}
\xrightarrow{\text{selection}}
\text{action}
\xrightarrow{\text{feedback}}
\text{updated strategy}

And that suggests a useful test for whether something deserves to be called a meta primitive:

Change the nouns, medium, scale, and perspective. Does the relation still make sense?

If threshold works for neurons, crowds, markets, chemical reactions, confidence, software load, and political movements, then “threshold” is probably sitting considerably deeper than any of those domains.

That’s where I’d quarry next: find the smallest relational alphabet from which the Pantheon’s larger operators can be composed. That would give you something closer to semantic machine code than another catalog of concepts.